fix(startup): stop the blank window flash before the first paint

On launch the main window became visible while React was still mounting,
so for a moment WebView2 showed an empty surface and the user saw a
translucent rectangle where the UI should be. Measured on this machine:
`ready` committed at 136ms, the window was revealed at 184ms only after
this change — before it, reveal raced ahead of the commit entirely.

Two independent fixes:

1. Reveal the window only after the first frame has actually painted.
   `app_show_main_window` used to fire before `ready` was committed. It now
   runs from a double requestAnimationFrame after the store update. One
   frame only guarantees the DOM was updated; two guarantee a paint.

2. Give html/body/#root a solid fallback colour.
   `background: rgb(var(--bg))` is an invalid declaration until the theme
   variables exist, and an invalid declaration is dropped outright — the
   surface was therefore transparent. `background-color: #0f111a` paints
   immediately; the themed `background` still wins once the variables
   resolve.

This is not the same root cause as the Installed-page ghost boxes, which
came from a synchronous disk scan (we_list_installed) blocking the main
thread. Here nothing blocks: the window simply appeared before its content
existed.

Gates: tsc 0, eslint 0, vitest 113, cargo test 52, clippy 0 warnings.